<h2>The anatomy of Section 18</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reference — Section 18(1).</strong> Any party to a dispute, with regard to an amount due under Section 17, may refer it to the Council. The provision opens with a non-obstante clause, displacing contrary forum arrangements.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conciliation — Section 18(2).</strong> The Council first conducts, or refers out, conciliation under the Mediation Act framework that now governs; no pre-deposit, no admissions, settlement possible at any point.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Arbitration — Section 18(3).</strong> If conciliation fails, the dispute goes to arbitration — by the Council itself or an institution it designates — as if under an arbitration agreement within the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Situs and speed — Sections 18(4) and 18(5).</strong> The supplier-location Council has jurisdiction; ninety days is the statutory aspiration for disposal.</div>
</div>

<h2>"Any party": real width, real limits</h2>
<p>The drafting choice is deliberate — the section could have said "the supplier may refer", and does not. Its width does useful work in at least three situations. A buyer served with a Samadhaan demand it considers inflated or fictitious need not wait passively: placing the dispute before the Council puts the computation in issue in the statutory forum rather than leaving the supplier to control the timing. Parties locked in parallel proceedings can use the reference to consolidate the delayed-payment component where the statute says it belongs. And where the question is who is a "supplier" at all — registration status, the trading question, enterprise classification — the Council hears that jurisdictional objection first, from whichever side raises it.</p>

<p>The limits are just as important. The anchor phrase — "with regard to any amount due under section 17" — keeps the Council a delayed-payment forum. It is not a general commercial court for the relationship: a buyer's damages claim for defective goods is a defence and counter-narrative within the reference, but as an independent claim it must find its own forum, subject to the ordinary rules on counterclaims in Section 18(3) arbitration. Nor does Section 18 manufacture liability where Chapter V does not reach — without a supply by a registered micro or small enterprise and an amount within Section 17, there is nothing to refer.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Drafting the reference (supplier side):</strong> plead the anchor explicitly — supply, invoice, appointed day, non-payment, Section 16 interest computation with monthly rests — and attach the Udyam certificate, purchase orders, invoices, delivery proof and the running account. References fail at the Council stage mostly for gaps in this chain, not for want of sympathy.</p>
</div>

<h2>Responding to a reference (buyer side)</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Take the conciliation stage seriously — it is the only stage without statutory interest accumulating in the background on a settlement figure, and the Council records what each side offered. Put jurisdictional objections (supplier status, registration timing, absence of supply) in writing at the first hearing, but prepare the merits alongside: an objection-only strategy that fails leaves the quantum record one-sided. Document quality disputes contemporaneously — debit notes raised at the time of supply carry weight that litigation-born grievances do not. And remember the exit toll: challenging an eventual award under Section 34 requires the 75 percent pre-deposit under Section 19, so the economics of settlement are best computed before the award, not after.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the forum keeps mattering</h2>
<p>Section 24 gives the delayed-payment chapter overriding effect, and the ninety-day aspiration — even loosely kept — makes the Council materially faster than a recovery suit. For micro and small suppliers in Delhi dealing with buyers across India, the supplier-location rule of Section 18(4) is itself a remedy: the dispute comes home. The "any party" phrase completes the design by making the forum common ground rather than one side's weapon — a feature parties on both sides of a delayed-payment dispute can use, provided they respect the Section 17 anchor that defines everything the Council can decide.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer is for general information only and is not legal advice. Forum questions in individual disputes depend on registration status, the supply chronology and the documents.</p>
</div>
